                <pb facs="1035" n="917" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg917"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.11173 Barley/55: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Poole</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-11-07T16:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 7,
                                1928—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 7—2:35
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>235. My 229, November 3, noon. A rather exact idea of the situation is provided
                    by following statistics just received from consul at Bremen respecting imports
                    and rejections of grade 2 barley: During September a total of 94,657 metric tons
                    arrived at Bremen [and] Weser ports of which 11,986 rejected; and during October
                    a total of 106,565 of which 32,247 rejected. Of foregoing, Gulf barley exempt
                    from import restrictions was September 20,606, October 37,844. Sixteen thousand
                    tons rejected barley are still at Weser ports.</p>
                <p>Exact figures for Hamburg are not available but situation there is the same as at
                    Bremen but on a good deal smaller scale.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Poole</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
